1
00:00:00,033 --> 00:00:03,816
<b>This is UN Resolution 181, the 1947 plan</b>

2
00:00:03,933 --> 00:00:06,533
<b>to split Palestine into two states, one</b>

3
00:00:06,533 --> 00:00:07,866
<b>Jewish and one Arab.</b>

4
00:00:08,533 --> 00:00:10,033
<b>It was the first time the international</b>

5
00:00:10,033 --> 00:00:12,316
<b>community voted on the region's future.</b>

6
00:00:12,816 --> 00:00:14,916
<b>To one side, it recognized the Jewish</b>

7
00:00:14,916 --> 00:00:16,833
<b>people's right to a sovereign state.</b>

8
00:00:17,383 --> 00:00:20,033
<b>To the other, foreign powers carved up a</b>

9
00:00:20,033 --> 00:00:20,883
<b>country that was already</b>

10
00:00:20,883 --> 00:00:21,883
<b>owned by the Palestinians.</b>

11
00:00:23,033 --> 00:00:25,016
<b>Almost nobody arguing about it has</b>

12
00:00:25,016 --> 00:00:26,916
<b>actually read the relevant documents.</b>

13
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:28,833
<b>When you do, the first</b>

14
00:00:28,833 --> 00:00:30,783
<b>surprise isn't about borders.</b>

15
00:00:31,166 --> 00:00:33,666
<b>Before the final vote, the Arab states</b>

16
00:00:33,683 --> 00:00:36,483
<b>argued that the UN had no legal authority</b>

17
00:00:36,516 --> 00:00:38,816
<b>under its own charter to partition a</b>

18
00:00:38,816 --> 00:00:40,733
<b>country against the will of its majority.</b>

19
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:44,433
<b>They motioned the World Court to rule on</b>

20
00:00:44,433 --> 00:00:46,050
<b>the legal validity of the plan.</b>

21
00:00:46,566 --> 00:00:47,849
<b>That motion was defeated</b>

22
00:00:47,849 --> 00:00:50,633
<b>by a single vote, 21 to 20.</b>

23
00:00:51,366 --> 00:00:53,616
<b>The partition resolution passed five days</b>

24
00:00:53,633 --> 00:00:55,916
<b>later, leaving its legal foundation</b>

25
00:00:55,916 --> 00:00:57,183
<b>permanently unsettled.</b>

26
00:00:58,083 --> 00:00:59,816
<b>This video is a look at the documents</b>

27
00:00:59,816 --> 00:01:01,650
<b>themselves, matching the popular</b>

28
00:01:01,683 --> 00:01:03,933
<b>narratives against the text and the facts</b>

29
00:01:03,933 --> 00:01:05,483
<b>historians actually agree on.</b>

30
00:01:06,233 --> 00:01:09,116
<b>By 1947, Britain had ruled Palestine for</b>

31
00:01:09,116 --> 00:01:10,349
<b>30 years under a</b>

32
00:01:10,349 --> 00:01:11,849
<b>League of Nations mandate.</b>

33
00:01:12,566 --> 00:01:14,433
<b>Decades of escalating conflict between</b>

34
00:01:14,433 --> 00:01:16,683
<b>the Arab and Jewish communities over</b>

35
00:01:16,683 --> 00:01:18,783
<b>immigration and land ownership had</b>

36
00:01:18,816 --> 00:01:19,616
<b>brought the territory</b>

37
00:01:19,616 --> 00:01:20,849
<b>to a breaking point.</b>

38
00:01:21,266 --> 00:01:22,266
<b>The standard Israeli</b>

39
00:01:22,283 --> 00:01:23,733
<b>narrative is straightforward.</b>

40
00:01:23,966 --> 00:01:26,150
<b>In November 1947, the</b>

41
00:01:26,150 --> 00:01:27,566
<b>UN offered a compromise.</b>

42
00:01:28,266 --> 00:01:29,783
<b>Two states for two peoples.</b>

43
00:01:30,483 --> 00:01:32,133
<b>The Jewish leadership accepted.</b>

44
00:01:32,583 --> 00:01:33,983
<b>The Arab leadership refused.</b>

45
00:01:34,666 --> 00:01:36,116
<b>The day after the British withdrew,</b>

46
00:01:36,483 --> 00:01:38,466
<b>neighboring Arab states invaded.</b>

47
00:01:38,883 --> 00:01:40,833
<b>One side chose compromise.</b>

48
00:01:41,016 --> 00:01:43,800
<b>The Arab side chose war and everything</b>

49
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:45,566
<b>that followed stems from that choice.</b>

50
00:01:46,616 --> 00:01:48,683
<b>The standard Arab narrative is different.</b>

51
00:01:48,900 --> 00:01:50,333
<b>For centuries, the land was</b>

52
00:01:50,333 --> 00:01:51,783
<b>overwhelmingly Arab.</b>

53
00:01:52,116 --> 00:01:54,216
<b>Following World War Two, a committee of</b>

54
00:01:54,216 --> 00:01:56,166
<b>foreign governments handed more than half</b>

55
00:01:56,166 --> 00:01:58,983
<b>of it to a minority population, drawing</b>

56
00:01:58,983 --> 00:02:00,900
<b>borders through communities that had</b>

57
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:02,483
<b>lived there for generations.</b>

58
00:02:03,066 --> 00:02:04,833
<b>In this version, the Arabs</b>

59
00:02:04,833 --> 00:02:06,783
<b>didn't reject a fair compromise.</b>

60
00:02:07,016 --> 00:02:08,966
<b>They refused to legitimize the partition</b>

61
00:02:08,966 --> 00:02:10,050
<b>of their own country.</b>

62
00:02:10,433 --> 00:02:12,483
<b>Neither narrative fully captures what was</b>

63
00:02:12,483 --> 00:02:13,916
<b>written in the plan itself.</b>

64
00:02:14,250 --> 00:02:15,383
<b>The Israeli version</b>

65
00:02:15,383 --> 00:02:17,633
<b>overlooks how fair the deal was.</b>

66
00:02:18,083 --> 00:02:20,333
<b>The Arab version overlooks if the plan</b>

67
00:02:20,333 --> 00:02:21,866
<b>could even be enforced.</b>

68
00:02:22,416 --> 00:02:23,166
<b>The core Arab</b>

69
00:02:23,166 --> 00:02:24,866
<b>objection was straightforward.</b>

70
00:02:25,083 --> 00:02:26,933
<b>They refused to accept being turned</b>

71
00:02:26,933 --> 00:02:28,199
<b>overnight into a</b>

72
00:02:28,199 --> 00:02:30,066
<b>minority, into a Jewish state.</b>

73
00:02:30,416 --> 00:02:31,466
<b>The numbers that are in</b>

74
00:02:31,466 --> 00:02:33,083
<b>the plan are uncontested.</b>

75
00:02:33,483 --> 00:02:36,483
<b>The UN resolution awarded 55% of the land</b>

76
00:02:36,566 --> 00:02:39,216
<b>to the Jewish state at a time when Jews</b>

77
00:02:39,233 --> 00:02:40,533
<b>made up roughly one third of the</b>

78
00:02:40,533 --> 00:02:42,333
<b>population and legally</b>

79
00:02:42,383 --> 00:02:44,283
<b>owned about 7% of the land.</b>

80
00:02:44,883 --> 00:02:47,166
<b>Defenders of the resolution argue these</b>

81
00:02:47,166 --> 00:02:48,666
<b>percentages are misleading.</b>

82
00:02:48,933 --> 00:02:52,650
<b>Most of that 55% was the Negev, a vast</b>

83
00:02:53,016 --> 00:02:54,633
<b>arid desert in the south.</b>

84
00:02:55,233 --> 00:02:57,266
<b>The Jewish state wasn't handed the prime</b>

85
00:02:57,333 --> 00:02:58,433
<b>asset of the country.</b>

86
00:02:59,033 --> 00:03:00,866
<b>It was handed the empty space.</b>

87
00:03:01,949 --> 00:03:03,783
<b>The Negev was poor farming land.</b>

88
00:03:04,050 --> 00:03:06,416
<b>That part is true, but it was only part</b>

89
00:03:06,416 --> 00:03:07,949
<b>of what the Jewish state got.</b>

90
00:03:08,433 --> 00:03:09,366
<b>The plan handed it the</b>

91
00:03:09,366 --> 00:03:10,866
<b>best farmland in the country.</b>

92
00:03:11,099 --> 00:03:13,533
<b>The coastal plain, the valleys, the</b>

93
00:03:13,533 --> 00:03:16,283
<b>citrus belt, and left the Arab state in</b>

94
00:03:16,283 --> 00:03:17,816
<b>the UN committee's own words.</b>

95
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:19,416
<b>Mountainous regions,</b>

96
00:03:19,466 --> 00:03:21,000
<b>largely uncultivable.</b>

97
00:03:21,666 --> 00:03:22,716
<b>And, even more</b>

98
00:03:22,716 --> 00:03:25,233
<b>importantly, the Negev was not empty.</b>

99
00:03:25,766 --> 00:03:28,383
<b>Britain's 1946 census counted over</b>

100
00:03:28,383 --> 00:03:30,983
<b>100,000 Bedouin Arabs in the Beersheba</b>

101
00:03:31,016 --> 00:03:33,833
<b>district, compared to just 1,000 Jews.</b>

102
00:03:34,416 --> 00:03:36,383
<b>The UN calculated that the proposed</b>

103
00:03:36,416 --> 00:03:38,916
<b>Jewish state would start with roughly</b>

104
00:03:38,916 --> 00:03:42,116
<b>500,000 Jews and 410,000 Arabs.</b>

105
00:03:42,383 --> 00:03:45,150
<b>However, those calculations excluded most</b>

106
00:03:45,150 --> 00:03:47,666
<b>of the Bedouin population by classifying</b>

107
00:03:47,666 --> 00:03:49,883
<b>them as nomads rather than residents.</b>

108
00:03:50,366 --> 00:03:51,666
<b>Include them and that</b>

109
00:03:51,666 --> 00:03:53,433
<b>Jewish majority disappears.</b>

110
00:03:53,766 --> 00:03:55,916
<b>The state designated as Jewish would have</b>

111
00:03:55,916 --> 00:03:57,783
<b>started off with an Arab majority.</b>

112
00:03:58,466 --> 00:04:00,333
<b>The fundamental issue wasn't the amount</b>

113
00:04:00,333 --> 00:04:02,283
<b>of land, but rather who</b>

114
00:04:02,283 --> 00:04:03,783
<b>would end up governing whom.</b>

115
00:04:04,683 --> 00:04:05,733
<b>The second structural</b>

116
00:04:05,733 --> 00:04:07,349
<b>flaw was more practical.</b>

117
00:04:07,949 --> 00:04:10,016
<b>The proposed map split the country into</b>

118
00:04:10,016 --> 00:04:12,533
<b>six different interwoven enclaves.</b>

119
00:04:12,933 --> 00:04:14,216
<b>Three for the Jewish state</b>

120
00:04:14,550 --> 00:04:16,016
<b>and three for the Arab state.</b>

121
00:04:16,650 --> 00:04:18,599
<b>Because Palestine had always functioned</b>

122
00:04:18,599 --> 00:04:20,133
<b>as a single economic unit,</b>

123
00:04:20,583 --> 00:04:22,949
<b>the UN tried to fix this fragmentation by</b>

124
00:04:22,949 --> 00:04:25,416
<b>forcing both states into a mandatory</b>

125
00:04:25,416 --> 00:04:26,849
<b>shared customs union.</b>

126
00:04:27,983 --> 00:04:30,150
<b>During the drafting phase, the UN</b>

127
00:04:30,150 --> 00:04:32,033
<b>committee appointed an independent</b>

128
00:04:32,033 --> 00:04:34,316
<b>economic expert, a certain Reedman,</b>

129
00:04:34,833 --> 00:04:36,383
<b>to assess whether these fractured</b>

130
00:04:36,416 --> 00:04:38,783
<b>territories could survive independently.</b>

131
00:04:39,233 --> 00:04:41,016
<b>His report was definitive.</b>

132
00:04:41,333 --> 00:04:44,066
<b>The Arab state would definitely not be</b>

133
00:04:44,066 --> 00:04:45,866
<b>viable without a customs union.</b>

134
00:04:46,283 --> 00:04:48,233
<b>Because the Jewish state was allocated to</b>

135
00:04:48,233 --> 00:04:50,250
<b>major commercial ports and the</b>

136
00:04:50,250 --> 00:04:51,833
<b>industrialized coastal plain,</b>

137
00:04:52,133 --> 00:04:53,733
<b>the Arab state was economically</b>

138
00:04:53,733 --> 00:04:55,116
<b>compromised from day one.</b>

139
00:04:55,349 --> 00:04:57,533
<b>It could only function if the neighbor it</b>

140
00:04:57,533 --> 00:04:58,650
<b>was partitioning from</b>

141
00:04:58,983 --> 00:05:00,716
<b>agreed to support its economy.</b>

142
00:05:01,766 --> 00:05:03,666
<b>When the Israeli side talks about the</b>

143
00:05:03,683 --> 00:05:06,366
<b>fair deal that the Arabs rejected, this</b>

144
00:05:06,366 --> 00:05:08,183
<b>is the deal that they are referring to.</b>

145
00:05:08,583 --> 00:05:10,683
<b>The biggest pro-Palestinian talking point</b>

146
00:05:10,683 --> 00:05:11,883
<b>about this plan is that</b>

147
00:05:11,883 --> 00:05:13,383
<b>it was imposed by the UN.</b>

148
00:05:13,949 --> 00:05:16,800
<b>But imposition requires binding legal</b>

149
00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:18,183
<b>authority and</b>

150
00:05:18,266 --> 00:05:21,083
<b>resolution 181 possessed none.</b>

151
00:05:21,566 --> 00:05:22,916
<b>The text itself reveals</b>

152
00:05:22,916 --> 00:05:24,333
<b>its legal limitations.</b>

153
00:05:24,716 --> 00:05:27,150
<b>The General Assembly recommends the</b>

154
00:05:27,150 --> 00:05:28,283
<b>partition plan to the</b>

155
00:05:28,283 --> 00:05:30,066
<b>mandatory power, Britain,</b>

156
00:05:30,383 --> 00:05:32,250
<b>and requests the Security</b>

157
00:05:32,250 --> 00:05:33,783
<b>Council to implement it.</b>

158
00:05:34,199 --> 00:05:36,033
<b>Under the UN Charter, the General</b>

159
00:05:36,033 --> 00:05:38,283
<b>Assembly holds no legislative power.</b>

160
00:05:38,616 --> 00:05:40,500
<b>Its resolutions are explicitly</b>

161
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:42,383
<b>non-binding recommendations.</b>

162
00:05:42,783 --> 00:05:44,433
<b>While the newly formed Israeli</b>

163
00:05:44,433 --> 00:05:45,516
<b>Provisional Government</b>

164
00:05:45,716 --> 00:05:47,250
<b>and the UN Secretary General</b>

165
00:05:47,516 --> 00:05:49,466
<b>later argued the resolution carried</b>

166
00:05:49,466 --> 00:05:51,033
<b>binding historical weight,</b>

167
00:05:51,449 --> 00:05:54,383
<b>the UN's own legal department formally</b>

168
00:05:54,383 --> 00:05:56,733
<b>concluded at the time that the text had</b>

169
00:05:56,766 --> 00:05:59,283
<b>no obligatory character whatsoever.</b>

170
00:06:00,150 --> 00:06:02,283
<b>It was a blueprint, not a law.</b>

171
00:06:03,066 --> 00:06:05,066
<b>That having said though, the United</b>

172
00:06:05,066 --> 00:06:07,416
<b>States exerted intense diplomatic and</b>

173
00:06:07,416 --> 00:06:09,483
<b>economic pressure on smaller nations</b>

174
00:06:09,750 --> 00:06:10,433
<b>to secure the</b>

175
00:06:10,433 --> 00:06:12,216
<b>necessary two-thirds majority.</b>

176
00:06:12,816 --> 00:06:15,300
<b>Liberia, heavily reliant on American</b>

177
00:06:15,300 --> 00:06:16,683
<b>rubber exports, was</b>

178
00:06:16,683 --> 00:06:18,683
<b>privately warned by US officials</b>

179
00:06:19,050 --> 00:06:20,433
<b>that corporate investments might be</b>

180
00:06:20,433 --> 00:06:21,983
<b>frozen if they voted no.</b>

181
00:06:22,416 --> 00:06:24,599
<b>The Philippines, who initially denounced</b>

182
00:06:24,599 --> 00:06:26,366
<b>the partition plan on the assembly floor</b>

183
00:06:26,366 --> 00:06:28,316
<b>as a violation of self-determination,</b>

184
00:06:28,733 --> 00:06:30,750
<b>changed their vote to yes after a</b>

185
00:06:30,750 --> 00:06:32,666
<b>pseudo-threatening telegram from US</b>

186
00:06:32,666 --> 00:06:34,199
<b>officials to their president.</b>

187
00:06:34,500 --> 00:06:35,666
<b>The pressure worked.</b>

188
00:06:35,933 --> 00:06:39,483
<b>Resolution 181 passed 33 to 13.</b>

189
00:06:40,316 --> 00:06:41,766
<b>But remember that the</b>

190
00:06:41,766 --> 00:06:43,866
<b>resolution was just a recommendation.</b>

191
00:06:44,183 --> 00:06:46,116
<b>A recommendation that could still be</b>

192
00:06:46,116 --> 00:06:48,566
<b>enforced if a major power provides the</b>

193
00:06:48,566 --> 00:06:50,099
<b>military power to back it up.</b>

194
00:06:50,583 --> 00:06:52,566
<b>But implementing partition against the</b>

195
00:06:52,566 --> 00:06:53,483
<b>will of the local</b>

196
00:06:53,483 --> 00:06:55,800
<b>majority required an army,</b>

197
00:06:56,099 --> 00:06:58,050
<b>and nobody was willing to send one.</b>

198
00:06:58,683 --> 00:07:00,866
<b>The resolution requested the UN Security</b>

199
00:07:00,866 --> 00:07:02,783
<b>Council to take over administration.</b>

200
00:07:03,483 --> 00:07:05,066
<b>But the council took no action.</b>

201
00:07:05,633 --> 00:07:07,349
<b>The small five-member commission</b>

202
00:07:07,349 --> 00:07:08,583
<b>dispatched to Jerusalem</b>

203
00:07:08,816 --> 00:07:10,133
<b>to oversee the transition</b>

204
00:07:10,383 --> 00:07:12,449
<b>quickly warned the UN that without an</b>

205
00:07:12,449 --> 00:07:13,583
<b>international force,</b>

206
00:07:13,766 --> 00:07:15,533
<b>the country would descend into open</b>

207
00:07:15,533 --> 00:07:17,016
<b>warfare the moment</b>

208
00:07:17,066 --> 00:07:18,366
<b>British forces withdrew.</b>

209
00:07:18,900 --> 00:07:20,883
<b>Britain was finally broken and military</b>

210
00:07:20,883 --> 00:07:22,500
<b>exhausted after World War II</b>

211
00:07:22,883 --> 00:07:24,783
<b>and refused to spend British lives</b>

212
00:07:24,783 --> 00:07:27,599
<b>enforcing a plan the Arab side rejected.</b>

213
00:07:28,116 --> 00:07:29,666
<b>They refused to manage an orderly</b>

214
00:07:29,666 --> 00:07:31,166
<b>handover, announced a</b>

215
00:07:31,166 --> 00:07:32,366
<b>hard departure date,</b>

216
00:07:32,666 --> 00:07:33,533
<b>and walked away.</b>

217
00:07:34,316 --> 00:07:35,916
<b>Meanwhile, the country that had been</b>

218
00:07:35,916 --> 00:07:36,633
<b>pressuring other</b>

219
00:07:36,633 --> 00:07:38,133
<b>nations to accept the plan</b>

220
00:07:38,316 --> 00:07:39,933
<b>had internally decided it would not</b>

221
00:07:39,966 --> 00:07:41,283
<b>intervene by force to</b>

222
00:07:41,316 --> 00:07:42,333
<b>implement that plan.</b>

223
00:07:43,050 --> 00:07:46,016
<b>11 days before the UN vote, the US State</b>

224
00:07:46,016 --> 00:07:47,216
<b>Department drafted a</b>

225
00:07:47,216 --> 00:07:48,566
<b>classified memorandum</b>

226
00:07:48,599 --> 00:07:49,433
<b>acknowledging that</b>

227
00:07:49,433 --> 00:07:51,066
<b>having championed partition,</b>

228
00:07:51,483 --> 00:07:52,616
<b>America was morally</b>

229
00:07:52,616 --> 00:07:54,366
<b>obliged to help to implement.</b>

230
00:07:55,283 --> 00:07:57,983
<b>Yet its final recommendation was to</b>

231
00:07:57,983 --> 00:07:59,933
<b>oppose the use of American troops.</b>

232
00:08:00,516 --> 00:08:02,333
<b>Washington calculated that military</b>

233
00:08:02,333 --> 00:08:04,199
<b>intervention would alienate the</b>

234
00:08:04,233 --> 00:08:06,083
<b>oil-producing Arab world and</b>

235
00:08:06,516 --> 00:08:07,566
<b>open a strategic door</b>

236
00:08:07,566 --> 00:08:08,883
<b>for Soviet intervention.</b>

237
00:08:09,833 --> 00:08:12,333
<b>The assembly recommended partition, but</b>

238
00:08:12,333 --> 00:08:14,250
<b>the nations with the power to enforce it</b>

239
00:08:14,583 --> 00:08:16,583
<b>had already quietly decided not to.</b>

240
00:08:17,166 --> 00:08:19,383
<b>This leaves the central political act.</b>

241
00:08:19,683 --> 00:08:20,883
<b>The Arab leadership's</b>

242
00:08:20,933 --> 00:08:22,583
<b>total rejection of the plan.</b>

243
00:08:22,949 --> 00:08:23,966
<b>Was that rejection a</b>

244
00:08:23,966 --> 00:08:25,683
<b>catastrophic strategic blunder,</b>

245
00:08:25,766 --> 00:08:26,633
<b>or was accepting the</b>

246
00:08:26,633 --> 00:08:28,283
<b>plan completely impossible?</b>

247
00:08:28,616 --> 00:08:29,933
<b>Historian Benny Morris</b>

248
00:08:29,933 --> 00:08:31,649
<b>frames it as an historic error.</b>

249
00:08:32,283 --> 00:08:34,233
<b>The international community offered the</b>

250
00:08:34,233 --> 00:08:35,366
<b>Arab population a</b>

251
00:08:35,366 --> 00:08:36,933
<b>recognized state of their own,</b>

252
00:08:37,116 --> 00:08:39,449
<b>backed by the legitimacy of the UN vote.</b>

253
00:08:39,750 --> 00:08:42,183
<b>By rejecting compromise, gambling on a</b>

254
00:08:42,183 --> 00:08:44,283
<b>military solution to block partition,</b>

255
00:08:44,583 --> 00:08:46,800
<b>and losing, they walked away with</b>

256
00:08:46,800 --> 00:08:48,833
<b>nothing, triggering the massive</b>

257
00:08:48,833 --> 00:08:50,616
<b>Palestinian refugee crisis.</b>

258
00:08:51,216 --> 00:08:53,550
<b>Historian Rashid Khalidi reads the</b>

259
00:08:53,550 --> 00:08:55,233
<b>document through a different lens.</b>

260
00:08:55,683 --> 00:08:57,533
<b>The Arab population constituted</b>

261
00:08:57,566 --> 00:08:58,949
<b>two-thirds of the country.</b>

262
00:08:59,583 --> 00:09:01,733
<b>Accepting partition meant volunteering to</b>

263
00:09:01,733 --> 00:09:03,483
<b>become a minority population</b>

264
00:09:03,600 --> 00:09:05,966
<b>within a self-defined Jewish state that</b>

265
00:09:05,966 --> 00:09:06,716
<b>was explicitly</b>

266
00:09:06,750 --> 00:09:08,633
<b>designed to prioritize mass</b>

267
00:09:08,666 --> 00:09:11,183
<b>Jewish immigration, meaning the Arab</b>

268
00:09:11,183 --> 00:09:13,433
<b>minority would permanently diminish.</b>

269
00:09:14,283 --> 00:09:15,449
<b>Khalidi points out that</b>

270
00:09:15,449 --> 00:09:17,483
<b>under decades of British rule,</b>

271
00:09:17,816 --> 00:09:20,016
<b>the Arab majority had been consistently</b>

272
00:09:20,033 --> 00:09:21,899
<b>denied national status,</b>

273
00:09:22,050 --> 00:09:24,716
<b>referred to in documents merely as the</b>

274
00:09:24,816 --> 00:09:26,433
<b>"non-Jewish communities."</b>

275
00:09:26,933 --> 00:09:28,800
<b>To him, the partition plan was the</b>

276
00:09:28,800 --> 00:09:30,416
<b>culmination of that erasure.</b>

277
00:09:30,983 --> 00:09:32,850
<b>This view deserves some pushback.</b>

278
00:09:33,266 --> 00:09:34,983
<b>The resolution itself did</b>

279
00:09:34,983 --> 00:09:36,683
<b>not erase the Arab population.</b>

280
00:09:37,050 --> 00:09:39,666
<b>It explicitly guaranteed an independent</b>

281
00:09:39,683 --> 00:09:41,883
<b>Arab state on nearly half the land,</b>

282
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:43,283
<b>and it mandated that the</b>

283
00:09:43,283 --> 00:09:45,483
<b>Jewish state included equal,</b>

284
00:09:45,816 --> 00:09:47,816
<b>non-discriminatory civil and political</b>

285
00:09:47,816 --> 00:09:50,100
<b>rights for its Arab minority within its</b>

286
00:09:50,100 --> 00:09:51,266
<b>future constitution.</b>

287
00:09:51,483 --> 00:09:53,066
<b>What the plan did demand the Arab</b>

288
00:09:53,066 --> 00:09:55,683
<b>majority to give up was not their rights,</b>

289
00:09:55,949 --> 00:09:58,500
<b>but their claim to the entire country.</b>

290
00:09:59,399 --> 00:10:00,383
<b>The Arab leadership</b>

291
00:10:00,383 --> 00:10:02,216
<b>supported a different resolution.</b>

292
00:10:02,666 --> 00:10:04,416
<b>The night of the vote, a competing</b>

293
00:10:04,433 --> 00:10:05,616
<b>proposal drafted by</b>

294
00:10:05,633 --> 00:10:07,949
<b>India, Iran, and Yugoslavia,</b>

295
00:10:08,216 --> 00:10:09,216
<b>was on the table.</b>

296
00:10:09,933 --> 00:10:12,300
<b>A single federal democratic state with</b>

297
00:10:12,300 --> 00:10:14,166
<b>autonomous Arab and Jewish regions</b>

298
00:10:14,516 --> 00:10:16,883
<b>under a unified constitution, but the</b>

299
00:10:16,883 --> 00:10:18,116
<b>partition resolution was</b>

300
00:10:18,116 --> 00:10:19,466
<b>called to the floor first,</b>

301
00:10:19,649 --> 00:10:21,416
<b>and once it passed, the federal</b>

302
00:10:21,416 --> 00:10:22,916
<b>alternative was discarded.</b>

303
00:10:23,466 --> 00:10:26,033
<b>We can, and historians have, spent</b>

304
00:10:26,133 --> 00:10:27,666
<b>decades debating whether</b>

305
00:10:27,666 --> 00:10:28,949
<b>the Arabs should have accepted</b>

306
00:10:28,949 --> 00:10:29,916
<b>the plan or not.</b>

307
00:10:30,233 --> 00:10:31,583
<b>In the end, the borders of</b>

308
00:10:31,583 --> 00:10:33,083
<b>the resolution never mattered.</b>

309
00:10:33,149 --> 00:10:35,016
<b>What did matter is that the resolution</b>

310
00:10:35,016 --> 00:10:37,316
<b>was the first time that the Jewish state</b>

311
00:10:37,566 --> 00:10:39,066
<b>got international recognition.</b>

312
00:10:39,483 --> 00:10:40,133
<b>What it did not</b>

313
00:10:40,133 --> 00:10:41,916
<b>create was a stable border.</b>

314
00:10:42,483 --> 00:10:45,266
<b>Those lines were drawn by the 1948 war.</b>

315
00:10:45,866 --> 00:10:47,966
<b>When the fighting paused, the territory</b>

316
00:10:47,966 --> 00:10:49,166
<b>under Israeli control</b>

317
00:10:49,500 --> 00:10:50,133
<b>grew from the</b>

318
00:10:50,133 --> 00:10:53,266
<b>proposed 55% to roughly 78%.</b>

319
00:10:54,633 --> 00:10:57,183
<b>The independent Arab state envisioned by</b>

320
00:10:57,183 --> 00:10:59,250
<b>the UN never came into being.</b>

321
00:10:59,766 --> 00:11:02,183
<b>Its remaining fragments were annexed or</b>

322
00:11:02,216 --> 00:11:04,116
<b>occupied by Jordan and Egypt.</b>

323
00:11:04,466 --> 00:11:06,933
<b>Both sides present simple stories that</b>

324
00:11:06,933 --> 00:11:07,883
<b>fall apart when you</b>

325
00:11:07,883 --> 00:11:09,383
<b>read the actual document.</b>

326
00:11:09,899 --> 00:11:12,683
<b>The 1947 plan was an unfair</b>

327
00:11:12,683 --> 00:11:15,266
<b>recommendation that had nobody behind it</b>

328
00:11:15,266 --> 00:11:16,800
<b>to enforce when it passed.</b>

329
00:11:17,666 --> 00:11:18,750
<b>If you want more history</b>

330
00:11:18,750 --> 00:11:20,433
<b>driven by the source documents,</b>

331
00:11:20,583 --> 00:11:21,366
<b>rather than the modern</b>

332
00:11:21,383 --> 00:11:24,000
<b>commentary, please subscribe.</b>
